Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 03:38:00 -0400 (EDT)
From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.UU.NET (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Economic systems

> I wrote the following essay.  I hope you find it interesting.

Interesting indeed.  Many of the assertions are consistent with what I
know of economic history (note: I'm not an expert), and it's a perspective
I hadn't seen before.

However, I think McEvedy's gloss of Herodotus to the effect that the Lydians
invented the market economy is sustainable only by selective ignorance.

There's plenty of evidence that the Sumerians (the first civilization of
which we have evidence) already had a flourishing market sector.  In particular,
the first evidence we have of writing is preserved commercial documents.  

Indeed, Sumerologists believe that cuneiform developed out of a system of
tally marks used to record barter contracts.  Here's how it worked.

Barter contracts, delivery orders, and invoices had originally taken the form
of small clay tokens representing so many sheep, so many ears of wheat, so
many yards of cloth, etc.  These were sealed in clay balls stamped with the
pictographic seals of the composing parties (for validation).  To read the
document, break the ball.

The problem with this was that it was a read-once medium.  So the custom
evolved of pressing tally marks and pictographs into the ball surface to match
the tokens inside.  That way you could read the document any number of times,
and if there were any doubt about the correctness of the tally marks, break it
and count the tokens.

Writing is what happened when they realized they could do without the tokens.

I've been to U.Penn and seen the working area of the group that's compiling
a dictionary of Sumerian.  Shelves stacked high with clay tablets thousands
of years old line the walls.

Are most of them royal decrees?  Love poetry?  Hymns to exotic gods?  Naaah...
90% are the ancient Sumerian equivalent of "This bill past due" or "I, Tur-Besh,
will deliver in the ninth month six fine rams to the premises of An-Kur..."

Sounds like a market economy to me.

On top of this, there's plenty of evidence of trade networks connecting
Sumer with Dilmun (modern Qatar) and points east, probably including the
Indus Valley civilizations at Mohenjo-Daro and Harappa.

OK, so they didn't have money.  They ran on barter and promissory notes,
probably using what we'd call futures contracts a lot.  It didn't seem
to slow them down much.
-- 
