(Scott DeLancey)

>>>Phonics advocates point out, reasonably, that
>>>that fact does not necessarily imply that that is the best way to
>>>start out learning to read.  As you develop fluency in and internalize
>>>any skill you bundle frequently-used sequences into subroutines that
>>>can be called as units--but it's a leap to infer from that that the
>>>best way to learn the skill is to skip the initial stage in which you
>>>learn the basic processes one at a time.

The other advantage of learning to read with phonics is that the child 
soon has a *reading* vocabulary of 5000 words which is the same size as 
his *spoken* vocabulary at age 6.  He already knows many words and can 
recognize them in print by sounding them out.  In Look-Say, they have 
to introduce words very slowly with lots of repetition in order to help 
memorization.  It makes for the most boring reading ever known to man.  
Phonics kids can read normal books right away and this helps them 
encounter new words in context and rapidly expand their reading vocabulary
beyond the 5,000 words they start with.  Look-Say Readers call for a 
basic vocabulary of 1000 words to be achieved at the end of 1 or 2 
*years* of instruction. 

Duncan Frissell

