Date: Mon, 12 Apr 93 14:17:22 EDT
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: EDU: American Intelligence

I think the discussion of phonics vs look-say misses the point. Reading is
a skill and, like all skills, needs to be practiced. If students read only
in school and the rare times that they do school related materials, they are
not going to skilled readers. Their vocabulary will be greatly lessened and
their ability to comprehend will also suffer.

This is not to say that Americans are less intelligent than earlier 
generations, their abilities in such areas dealing with the written
word as well as problem solving which are the mainstay of intelligence
measuring tests are less. Personally, I would consider that to be less
intelligent because I can not find new abilities that are taking the
old ones place. (This is just personal experience as a tutor, teaching
assistant, and computer consultant.)

Another problem I would note is more of philosophy than educational practice.
Too many current educational philosophies revolve around really mushy 
relativism where the correctness of an answer is less important that the
student's participation. (i.e. self-esteem garbage) While this has its
place, it is not conducive to developing abilites in areas where there are
definite answers like math or science. It is also damaging to skeptical
thought and reason.

I am speaking only for myself here, but in teaching undergraduates at a
large state university I have noticed the lack of the skills I mentioned
above. Following simple English instructions in a biology lab book
is beyond many students. In particular, dealing with previously un-experienced
phenomena is a skill nearly all my students lack. When confronted with a
situation of a series of repetitive experiments where specific
instructions were given only for the first couple of repititions, around
75% of the students were unable to extrapolate and continue the experiment.
I consider such skills to be part of any definition of intelligence, but
whether the precense of this ability is declined I can't postively say.
Personally, I would say yes.

A.

