Mike suggests:
>I take your point about the neutral zone being rather too large to
>search.  Perhaps a more natural Schelling point would be the destruction
>site(s).  The wreckage from their hole will now be in *your* empire time
>and yours in *theirs*.  You each know the point of destruction of your
>own hole, the shape of your local empire time cone and, from the
>direction of the debris of the alien hole, the place of destruction of
>the enemy hole can be inferred.  If each side sends a hole to each
>destruction site (but more slowly, this time) communications should be
>easily established.

Assume the wormhole ends are all in a line in space, and each wormhole
consists of one end fixed at the empire center, and one end moving out
very fast. Then the spacetime diagram of a collide scenario is:
             
               
                B  D
                /\/\       Fixed
  Fixed        / /\ \       End       time
   End        / /  \ \       |         ^
    |        / /    \ \burst |         |
    |  burst/ /      \ \     |         * - > space
    |      / /        \ \    |
    |     / /          \ \   |
    |    / /            \ \  |
    |   / /              \ \ |
    |  / /        Moving  \ \|  
    | / /           End   |  | C
  A |/ / Moving            \ |
    |  |  End              | | 
    | /                     \|
    | |                      *
    |/                    Hole 2 made
    *
 Hole 1 made

As the moving ends reach events B and D, a burst of vaccum
fluctuations flys out from fixed end 1 at event A, enters moving end 2
at event B, flys out from fixed end 2 at event C, enters moving end 1
at event D, and flys out fixed end 1 at event A to close the time
loop.  This burst is enough to close the smaller of the holes, and may
or may not be enough to close the other hole also.

So just as you see your home hole end suddenly close, a burst of stuff
flies out of it toward the destruction site of the other hole,
wiggling a minimim path through all the intermediate mass
distributions.  This is your only clue to where their hole end was
destroyed (or hit).  You'd have great difficulty measuring this angle
accurately enough, you'd need to know enough about the distribution of
mass inbetween, and you still don't know how old their hole is in
their empire time in order to determine how much closer to you they
were hit than where your hole was destroyed.

Going slower the next time doesn't solve the problem that the holes
threaten to form a causal loop if they cross each other.  And going
slower takes up empire time.

Still seems to me that you'd do best to pick out a big quazar and both
head to that.  However, it's not clear to me when you'd want to:

Imagine that we sent out holes as soon as we were able and they
collided 25 empire years later.  Would we *want* to try hard to meet
up with the aliens soon after that?  If we waited another 100-1000
years our civilization might grow much larger and stronger, and able
to stand up to possibly hostile aliens.  Brash civilizations that
choose to meet up as soon as they are able might be easy prey for
those that wait until their abilities have reached more of a plateu.
One might be wise to *not* meet up until very well defended.  

And of course we would have absolutely nothing to go on to estimate
the abilities and freindliness of aliens until we met them, at which
point it would be too late.  If I wrote sf on this, I'd have all these
unknown alien contacts, that we've been too scared to follow up on
yet.  We'd *think* we'd closed all the holes in those areas fast
enough, but strange things keep happening :-).

