Date: Mon, 12 Apr 93 13:37:18 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Extropian Priorities

Here is how I modelled the tech singularity:

First, I note that the human brain has about 10^11 neurons, 10^4
synapses/neuron, and a signalling rate of 100 bits/sec per synapse,
for an overall rate of 10^17 bits/sec.  Let us not quibble about the
exact value, since it only alters the date of the singularity by a
few years if I am off by an order of magnitude.

An engineer is worth about $1 million in present value (roughly),
so to match an engineer in raw processing power you need 10^11
bits/sec/$.  Now a human only works 25% of the time, while a
machine might be expected to work 100% of the time, so for
human-equivalent utility you need maybe 25 billion bits/sec/$.

Today, a personal computer provides 25,000 bits/sec/$, so we want
a factor of a million increase.  Since computer power/$ is
increasing by two times every two years, or 1000 times in
20 years, we get the required increase of 1 million times in
40 years, or 2033 A.D.

So, at that time you would expect human-equivalent raw computer
power available to support engineers in their work, moving
engineering productivity up the scale.  If we assume that engineers
get their workstations upgraded over several years, then we add
a couple of years to when the engineering capability doubles.

Now for some modifying assumptions:

I assumed that it takes a machine with the raw power of a human
brain to provide human-equivalent support (i.e. doing the work
of a junior engineer).  If this is an over-estimate, then the
design cycle could get hopped up quicker.

If a typical design drawing takes a couple of megabytes to store,
and on the order of 100,000 drawings can describe a self-replicating
factory (macroscopic, not nanoscale), then you need 200 Gbytes.
This is rapidly coming into the range of data storage devices,
so near or fully replicating factories may appear in the near
future, also jacking up technical progress.

The developed world is spreading into other countries, incresing
the total number of engineers and scientists working on stuff.
This is a positive factor.  On the other hand, new knowledge
may only be discoverable at a less-than-linear rate as a function
of research effort.

What I assumed in my model was as follows:

- New knowledge and technology progress is a logarithmic function
of the effort applied.  So if 100 times as much effort is applied,
you get 4.6 times as much progress.

- The number of engineers & scientists will increase at 3% per
year as more of the world joins the 'developed' world.  By the
projected date of the singularity, some of the world will still
be 'undeveloped'.

- It takes a human-equivalent in raw computer power to add one
human-equivalent in research/development.

What I get is this:

Over the next 30 years, the number of engineers and scientists
increases to 2.4 times today's value, and the average rate of
progress is 40% higher than today.  We expect human-equivalent
machines to arrive at about this time, i.e. 2023AD.  We assume
that 25% of the workstations are upgraded per year, so by 2025AD
we are adding machines at a total capacity equal to the human
workforce each year.  A few years before this, most of the increase
in the rate of progress was due to the increase in human
engineers and scientists.  After this point, the increase is
dominated by the machines.  After 5 more years, we are up to
100x the engineering workforce in computer power, and the rate
of progress has picked up to 4 times today's rate (i.e. a computer
should be doubling in power every 6 months).  If I allow for
some programming lag in using these machines effectively,
and round off, I get the year 2035.

Dani Eder

