   From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.uu.net (Eric S. Raymond)

   There's plenty of evidence that the Sumerians (the first
   civilization of which we have evidence) already had a flourishing
   market sector.  In particular, the first evidence we have of
   writing is preserved commercial documents.

   Are most of them royal decrees?  Love poetry?  Hymns to exotic
   gods?  Naaah...  90% are the ancient Sumerian equivalent of "This
   bill past due" or "I, Tur-Besh, will deliver in the ninth month six
   fine rams to the premises of An-Kur..."

   Sounds like a market economy to me.

   On top of this, there's plenty of evidence of trade networks
   connecting Sumer with Dilmun (modern Qatar) and points east,
   probably including the Indus Valley civilizations at Mohenjo-Daro
   and Harappa.

   OK, so they didn't have money.  They ran on barter and promissory
   notes, probably using what we'd call futures contracts a lot.  It
   didn't seem to slow them down much.

Yes ... and no.  I forgot to include a paragraph or two.  An
interesting thing about the development of a market economy is that it
seemed to go in phases.  The first market transactions seem to be
things of significant value, long-distance trading, and the like.  As
far as I can tell, Herodotus is right (strictly read), in that
*retail* trade in the daily necessities is relatively new; old
cultures used the markets fairly infrequently, as do the few remaining
subsistance farmers in the US.

(If you look closely, the market gots its start in areas where the
high transaction costs weren't a barrier, or the existing traditional
systems didn't get the job done.  Money is a prime example of
technology enabling markets.)

Also, many of these transactions seem to not have the crucial element
of well-defined prices.  (According to the law codes I've read,)
prices seemed to be set by custom or law in a *lot* of transactions,
or the payment was in-kind in a way that would have made it difficult
to make a "market" of the transaction.  For instance, it seems that if
I had a piece of land and wanted to hire someone to plant an orchard
on it, the wage was assumed to be half-ownership of the resulting
orchard.  (The law declared how to handle the case where the worker
didn't finish the job.)

You also have to be careful interpreting documents.  A promise to
deliver 6 rams and 6 ewes in six months doesn't necessarily mean that
they've been bought.  They could be the inventory of a flock that a
shepherd has been hired to guard, with the wage being set at the
increase over the original herd.  Similarly, long-range trade can be
accomplished via gift-giving exchanges.

I do not endorse McEvedy's idea that markets did not exist before
money, but they seem to have grown up very slowly (even the Yanamamo
have a few almost-true market transactions), and to have only become
the dominant form of transaction in urbanized societies with decent
transportation and coined money.

BTW, one intriguing aspect of ancient Mesopotamian culture is its
intense legalism and formalism.  It is common for a document to have
five witnesses, stating that four people declare that Mr. X did
such-and-so.  Given that nine peole were involved in creating the
document, the expense must have been immense!  Buying and selling a
few sheep seems to have involved more risk of repudiation of the
contract than buying a care involves these days.

Dale

