Subject: FTL/BOOK: traversable wormholes

I've just finished reading "Timemaster" by Robert Forward.  Forward is
a good hard sf writer, and tries hard to get all his technical detail
solid, though he isn't the greatest story teller.  This book is about
using wormholes for FTL and time travel (he presumes wormhole time
machines are possible, contrary to current indications).

Forward doesn't much consider the social/economic/military
implications (and what he does include doesn't seem particularly well
thought out), though he does have a cute way to make use of info about
the future while still allowing folks to influence that future.

Forward is so focused on telling a space travel story, with lone
courageous pilots in their small fast dangerous machines, that he
misses an obviously better approach.  Forward has each ship holding
the end of a hole, used to move supplies into the ship from home, and
to talk with home, and has the speed of the ship limited by the
radiation the pilot can stand.  But why not leave the pilot at home,
who then controls the ship through data sent through the hole?  No
more need to have the pilot there than people now need to sit in front
of the windshields on their cars.

Robin Hanson

