From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: LAW: Employment contracts

   From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)

   It's an imperfect system, but then humans are rather far
   from perfect themselves.  Is there a better way to measure informed consent
   than by having people attest that they knowingly agree?  Keep in mind that
   further safeguards (such as consenting to consenting to . . .) have
   decreasing marginal use but nearly constant marginal costs.  And mindscans
   are not yet an option.

(I don't mean to be arguing fundamentals here.)  In practice, informed
consent isn't really good enough.  People tend to under-estimate the
importance of rare events (except for things which have gotten a lot
of press!), and so contracts often forget to include provisions for
rare but expensive problems:  marriage contracts don't provide for
divorce, auto purchase contracts don't allow for resolving problems
with "lemons", medical services rarely pre-negotiate dealing with
questions of malpractice.  (Legal systems often try to patch up these
problems ex post facto.  For instance, in one Mespoptamian law code,
marriage prices were left to individual negotiation, but divorce
prices were fixed by law.  The Jews have solved this by establishing a
custom that marriage contracts are *required* to have divorce
provisions.)

Of course, once "society" starts taking care of rare, future events,
people have even less incentive to worry about them.

Is there a way to increase people's rationality in dealing with these
issues?  Or are there fundamental limitations on human rationality
that put limits on what can be intelligently contracted?

Dale

