Date: Mon, 12 Apr 93 16:19:48 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: Sex & Free Love/Liberation

Here's my reply to Jim Kalb's article about sexual liberation, etc.
I couldn't help myself.  I've taken out the quotation of his whole letter;
my response was (almost) all in a block, not interspersed with his stuff.

> Newsgroups: alt.revolution.counter
> From: jk@panix.com (Jim Kalb)
> Subject: Sex and Free Love
> Organization: Institute for the Human Sciences
> Date: Sun, 28 Feb 1993 12:28:56 GMT

Dear Mr. Kalb,

I've quoted a posting of yours below [omitted].  I'm not on the newsgroup 
but got it indirectly.  Feel free to quote my reply, but please do so in
full if you do.  I didn't have time for a point-by-point reply, but I 
thought it would be good to summarize my response to your premises, 
which as I understand them are:

1) As a society, we should try to encourage good relations between
   men and women.
2) As a society, we should encourage the having and raising of
   children.
3) As a society, we want children when they grow up to be responsible to 
   their parents.
4) Given freedom, people will choose evil over good.
5) In freedom, people are focused more on immediate satisfaction.
6) Women are significantly more naturally attached to their children
   than men.
7) In order that children be raised well, we want their biological 
   fathers to stick around.

You may have guessed by my wording that I disagree with all of these 
premises.  With some more strongly than others.

1) Perhaps it would be tragic for male/female relations to worsen as
   a whole.  I have a hard time imagining what my response to such a
   hypothetical situation would be, but I think I would say, let each 
   of us fools work out the problem on his or her own, especially me.
2) Sexual liberation is naturally helped by the current obsolescence
   of childrearing.  *If* we were suffering from a lack of population
   (quaint thought!) the problem would be naturally self-correcting.
   *Quality,* as opposed to quantity of childrearing is important,
   and I think people who have chosen the role *against* prevailing
   custom are more likely to be suited to it than people who have 
   been pushed into it.  But in any case it's for them to find out.
3) Given that this is not necessary in a practical sense, we're down
   to sentiment.  If children don't feel attachment in their hearts 
   to their parents, then what is the point of wanting them to act
   as if they did?
4) This is the crux of most of your arguments, of course.  I wonder 
   how seriously you want to be taken, whether you're intensionally
   exaggerating in order to fish for a good reply.  I don't think
   any inspired quip will convince people to want freedom.  Making
   choices is the only way to learn, and until people have learned, 
   they play a weird game of asking for restrictions and then 
   pushing against them.  Teething on authority instead of chewing 
   on life.  I'm not interested in chewing or offering the pacifier.
5) I would say that the person living under sanctions is more 
   concerned with the present and that nothing draws your thoughts
   out to the future like having no one to ask what to do.  Compare
   "What will people think?" to "What do I want?" for enticement
   to short-sightedness.  (Well, you did, but think again.)
6) I have no facts to back up my reaction to this fact-proof 
   assertion.  But it does weaken your argument.  Your
   argument for couples and families staying together when nature 
   won't hold them is tenuous enough without your proposing the
   additional strain of having the members of the family fit
   certain roles within it!  Sex roles are irrelevant to your
   argument for social enforcement of family responsibilities.
7) I only disagree that lack of fathers is such a tragedy that we
   should draft unwilling men into the job.  In any case, the problem
   is that fathers and mothers are presently, statistically, too 
   *different* from each other.  (And the problem of bad/missing 
   parents is not so clearly gender-determined as you make it seem.)

Given such disagreement with your premises, I haven't much to say
about your conclusions.  But there's an interesting deductive step 
you take.  You say that we should fashion sex roles to fit people's 
natures.  As a consequence, you argue that men should be encouraged to 
be loyal fathers.  This after arguing that they naturally are not.  
After you've knocked yourself out like this, all I can think to offer 
is a cold compress.  I wonder how fathers reading your article feel.
   
I agree somewhat with one thing you say:

> Moreover, the government can contribute to a restoration simply by
> ceasing to undermine traditional sex roles.  Anti-discrimination
> measures, and the welfare state generally, are intended (among other
> things) to make women economically independent of particular men and
> therefore to help realize the goals of women's liberation.  Abandonment
> of such measures would contribute to a better life for all by helping
> knock the props out from under the illusion of equality.

I agree that government should leave people to themselves to find what 
values and ways of satisfying them they can.  Let your views and mine
and everybody else's compete on equal footing and I can respect you.

You seem to view human nature (as many do) as a battleground between 
forebrain and hindbrain, with no heart in between, but requiring external 
force as a substitute.  I would like to ignore the combination of sadness
and futile silliness there, but it's also a poison that self-doubt 
leaves us open to.  You have to count on people's hearts, not only
because a world of substitutes for hearts is pointless, but because hearts 
don't grow until someone counts on them.

One last thing.  As I got to the end I wondered, where did the subject of
sex disappear to?
 
--Steve Witham
